WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:26.491
[Meet CineTray]
Hi there, and welcome to CineTray! CineTray is a tiny Chrome extension that turns saving YouTube videos into a single, delightful gesture. Instead of opening menus and hunting for buttons, you simply press a video, drag it onto a dock, and drop it into Watch Later or any playlist you like. Let me walk you through everything, step by step.

2
00:00:27.791 --> 00:00:48.122
[Why it exists]
Saving a video the normal way takes three clicks: open the three-dot menu, choose Save to Watch Later, then pick a playlist. And there is nothing visual about it. CineTray replaces all of that with one smooth drag-and-drop motion, and it makes your collection feel alive while you build it.

3
00:00:49.422 --> 00:01:18.242
[Install in Chrome]
Installing CineTray takes less than a minute, and you can watch me do it right here. First, download the project folder from GitHub. Then open Chrome, and type chrome dash extensions into the address bar. On that page, flip on Developer mode, exactly like this. Click Load unpacked, pick the folder you downloaded, and CineTray appears in your list, ready to go.

4
00:01:19.542 --> 00:01:29.525
[The gesture]
Now for the fun part. Press any video and move a little: the card lifts and follows your cursor, and the dock slides in from the right.

5
00:01:30.825 --> 00:01:47.352
[The dock]
The dock is your filing cabinet. Glide across it with me: Watch Later with a live count, then a well for every playlist, each one glowing as the card comes close. And the dashed well at the bottom is where new playlists are born.

6
00:01:48.652 --> 00:01:58.935
[Create a playlist mid-drag]
You can even create a playlist without letting go: hover the dashed well, type a name, press Enter, and the card files itself straight in.

7
00:02:00.235 --> 00:02:13.223
[Cancel and undo]
Changed your mind? Escape flies the card home, nothing saved. And watch this: drop one in, press Undo, and it hops right back out of the list.

8
00:02:14.523 --> 00:02:28.375
[The hover chip]
Prefer one click saving? Rest your cursor on a video card, and a small plus button appears in the corner. One click saves the video straight to Watch Later, complete with sparkles.

9
00:02:29.675 --> 00:02:43.372
[Right-click saving]
You can also right click any video link or thumbnail, and choose CineTray from the menu. Save it to Watch Later, or straight into one of your playlists, without even hovering the card first.

10
00:02:44.672 --> 00:02:58.105
[Player long-press]
On a watch page, you do not even need the list around. Press and hold inside the video player for half a second, and the current video lifts up, ready to drop into any well in the dock.

11
00:02:59.405 --> 00:03:20.701
[The dashboard]
The dashboard is mission control. Open it from the toolbar, pick Watch Later, and browse everything you filed. Rows lift as you hover, and drag to reorder. And in Settings, one click flips the dock to the left side of the screen and back. Export and import live here too.

12
00:03:22.001 --> 00:03:36.339
[Popup and badge]
The popup is the quick glance: total saves, and every playlist. Watch me type a new playlist name right here, and it appears instantly. The toolbar badge counts the whole collection.

13
00:03:37.639 --> 00:03:57.285
[Privacy]
One important thing. CineTray is completely local. Your playlists live inside your own browser. No accounts, no sign in, and the extension never talks to any server. Export your collection any time. The file is yours.

14
00:03:58.585 --> 00:04:16.137
[Outro]
And that is CineTray! Press, drag, drop, and enjoy your beautifully organized video shelf. The full project, with this walkthrough video and the complete documentation, is on GitHub. Happy collecting!

